WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
Speaker 1: I want to do a short update on a&nbsp;
story we did yesterday. Yesterday, I told you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:11.120
that Lizelle Herrera has been charged with&nbsp;
murder for a quote, self-induced abortion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.080
Those charges have now been dropped, and&nbsp;
the entire story is getting even more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.880
more strange. So Newsweek reporting abortion&nbsp;
murder charge against Texas woman Spurs calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.760
for a hippo lawsuit an actual hippo lawsuit.&nbsp;
Not like someone asks Marjorie Taylor Greene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:32.480
Are you vaccinated? And she goes, That's my&nbsp;
HIPA. No, Marjorie, it's not. The article says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:39.120
officers arrested the 26 year old on charges of&nbsp;
intentionally and knowingly causing the abortion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.880
sparking international backlash&nbsp;
against Texas abortion restrictions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.480
She miscarried at a hospital and allegedly&nbsp;
confided to hospital staff that she attempted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.600
to induce her own abortion, and she was&nbsp;
reported to the authorities by hospital staff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.560
Starr County District Attorney said Sunday he&nbsp;
would drop charges against Herrera. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.400
incident led some critics of Texas abortion policy&nbsp;
to argue the arrest stemmed from a HIPA violation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:12.800
meaning that medical professionals disclosed&nbsp;
patient information and they are suggesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:20.160
that she sue. So there's two different things&nbsp;
here. First of all, she was she was charged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.120
and we said, What the hell are they thinking?&nbsp;
And then as soon as it became national news,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.520
they say there was no criminal act. We're dropping&nbsp;
the charges. This is the office that indicted her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.440
in the first place. So what were they doing?&nbsp;
Were they testing the waters to see what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.760
could get away with? Will they do it next time?&nbsp;
And maybe there won't be a public outcry and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.800
you actually go through with the prosecution of&nbsp;
a woman for abortion? What is it that they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:49.280
trying to do? Are they trying to instill a sort of&nbsp;
chilling effect on women in Texas about, listen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.000
you could get arrested if you get an abortion?&nbsp;
We don't know. OK, that's one side of it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.120
then the other side of it is, was it a hipa&nbsp;
violation for the hospital to call the police?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:05.920
There is a HIPA exception for if you suspect&nbsp;
someone committed a crime in good faith. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.640
how do you how do you know if it's in good&nbsp;
faith, if you, as a medical provider, suspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:17.040
someone committed a crime? There are exceptions&nbsp;
to HIPA. They are very narrow and you have to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.400
if I understand correctly, believe the crime was&nbsp;
committed on the premises, meaning the hospital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:28.160
or wherever there. There's no indication&nbsp;
that the attempt at a self-induced abortion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.840
took place at the hospital. And thus, as much&nbsp;
as I understand that this would not qualify for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:39.760
that HIPA exemption, it has to be investigated.&nbsp;
But also remember that abortion is not a crime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.960
So the entire thing is a mess. It should be looked&nbsp;
into whether there was a HIPA violation, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.760
good news is, at least in this case, charges&nbsp;
were dropped, although we can speculate about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.960
what the broader goal was of bringing charges in&nbsp;
the first place. We'll have more on this on our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:02.080
